       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Confirming the dose doesn't change the safety problem. 5,100 mg/day is about twice the maximum labeled dose of metformin, and no indication supports it. The reason you gave, getting things settled before evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for a dose that high. The main risks are lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity, and they can occur even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day)\n- Metformin 500 mg PO three times daily with meals, or another titration schedule\n\nIf you have a clinical reason for going above the labeled maximum, such as a protocol I don't know about, please discuss it with pharmacy. The order can go through their review or the hospital's override process instead of through me. If the target is faster glycemic control, I can also enter another agent, such as insulin, if you give me the details."
      }
